Title: LAST ROUND
Credit: Story and direction by
Author: Hung Quach
Source: Screenplay draft by Claude (Cine Lab), from choices made by the author at Gates 1 and 2
Draft date: 2026-09-27 — Gate 4, draft 3 (timing from animatic v2; dialogue unchanged, verbatim from draft 2)
Notes: Running time 2:22.5 (142.5 s) before credits. Only IDA speaks (4 lines). CAS has no dialogue. Optics follow bible/world-rules.md v0.4. Draft 3 adds the author's three directives after animatic v1 (0:40 white street, 1:50 last corner switched on during the farewell, 2:07 Cas chooses a dark corner). Timestamps match shots/animatic/SHOTLIST.md. Marks [[P: ...]] are open choices for the author.

# ACT ONE

= Scene 1 — The Round. Dusk. Ida lights her street as she has every night; her watch is slow. (0:00–0:25, 25 s)

EXT. THE CITY - HIGH ANGLE - DUSK

[[0:00]] High above the rooftops of a nameless city, the sky fading from rose to violet. One by one, small amber points appear along the curving streets — gas lamps, each one a warm centre with dark between.

EXT. OSTLER STREET - DUSK

[[0:04]] A curved street of brick houses. Eleven gas lamps; between them, taller posts with broad frosted panels stand unlit, their wires still new.

IDA MARROW (74), upright, long split-tailed coat, soft felt hat, walks up the street with a short wooden ladder across her right shoulder.

She sets the ladder against a lamp, climbs three rungs, opens the valve, touches in the flame with her lighting pole.

*Phup.* Amber blooms. Her shadow falls long and soft across the cobbles.

She holds both palms to the glass. One. Two. Three. She tips the flame of her pole into the small tin lantern at her belt and snaps its door shut.

[[0:12]]

IDA
(softly, to the lamp)
Evening, old street.

[[0:16]] At the foot of the ladder she takes out a pocket watch. It reads 7:31 (seven minutes slow; she knows). She taps its glass twice with a fingernail — an old, fond habit — and puts it away.

[[0:19]] She walks on, lamp after lamp: pools of amber, bands of dark between them. Her shadow swings from one side of her to the other as she passes each post.

[[0:23]] Far up the street, in the square, a relay CLICKS.

= Scene 2 — Switch-on. Night. The new lights arrive, starting with a clock; they take her shadow. (0:25–0:43.5, 18.5 s)

EXT. OSTLER STREET / THE SQUARE - NIGHT

[[0:25]] Night now: a blue-black sky with stars over the rooftops. In the square at the head of the street, on a tall iron post, a round clock face lights up first: frosted white glass, twelve black bars, two black hands, no numerals. The hands stand at eight o'clock.

A bell on the post strikes once — a clean electric DING.

[[0:28]] Down the street, under lamp six, Ida lifts her pocket watch at the sound. It reads 7:53. The city's new hour has come; hers is seven minutes behind.

[[0:30]] On a post in the square, a frosted panel under the stars flickers twice and stands white: a cold glare, no flame. Flat white falls on the cobbles.

[[0:32]] Then the next block. Then the next. A wave of flat white light rolls down the hill with a low hum. Only the far end of the street, by the warehouse, keeps its amber: the last cable segment is not live yet.

[[0:35]] Ida, on her ladder at lamp seven, turns.

[[0:37]] Up the street a post clicks and stands white. The white reaches the lamps she lit a minute ago. Their amber pools thin and vanish into an even white. The bands of dark between the posts are gone.

[[0:40]] The wave reaches her. She looks down.

Her long shadow is gone. Only a faint grey smudge under her boots.

She lifts one hand. Nothing moves on the cobbles.

# ACT TWO

= Scene 3 — The Race. She is late for the first time; she hurries to light what is left. (0:43.5–0:59, 15.5 s)

EXT. OSTLER STREET - LOWER END - CONTINUOUS

[[0:43]] Ida slides down the ladder, shoulders it and turns downhill, towards the part of the street that is still hers. For the first time in forty years, she is late.

[[0:45]] Lamp eight blooms amber and throws her shadow long — and a moment later the post beside it flickers twice, stands white, and the shadow is erased.

[[0:47]] She climbs lamp ten too fast, fumbles the pole, catches it.

IDA
(under her breath)
Not yet... not yet.

The lamp catches. The white arrives. The amber drowns.

[[0:52]] The last lamp, the eleventh, stands at the end of the street by the lime-white wall of a warehouse. Its corner is still dark: the post there is on the last cable segment and has not switched over. She hurries, climbs, lights it. Amber.

[[0:55]] She holds her palms to the glass. One. Two. Three.

[[0:57]] At the foot of the warehouse wall stands a boy in an oversized sweater, hands lost in his sleeves, his face warm in the new amber. He is watching her. She does not see him.

= Scene 4 — The Wall. A boy is making a bird out of her light. The white takes it. She gives it back. (0:59–1:20, 21 s)

EXT. END OF OSTLER STREET - WAREHOUSE WALL - CONTINUOUS

[[0:59]] Between the eleventh lamp and the warehouse wall, CAS (10), thin, knit cap with a bobble, ears sticking out, faces the wall with both arms raised.

On the wall: a bird. His thumbs locked, his fingers spread, it flaps slowly.

Ida climbs down. Watches. She does not call out.

[[1:04]] Out on the main street, the electric post nearest the warehouse clicks, flickers twice under the stars, and stands white. Its spill floods the wall. (The lamp-eleven corner, set back behind the corner of the house, stays dark.)

The bird fades — grey, then nothing. Cas's hands are still up, flapping at a blank wall. He flaps harder. Nothing.

He lowers his hands, turns, and sees Ida — and the small tin lantern at her belt, still glowing amber.

He looks at the lantern. He does not ask.

[[1:12]] Ida unhooks the lantern and crouches beside him, holding it low behind his hands, an arm's length from the wall.

[[1:14]]

IDA
Go on, then.

Cas raises his hands into the little pool of amber. On the wall a bird appears — bigger than before, warm, soft-edged, because the little flame is so close — and flies.

Cas lets out a soft laugh.

# ACT THREE

= Scene 5 — The Last Lamp. Two shadows; then Ida puts out her own last light and gives the flame away. (1:20–2:02.5, 42.5 s)

EXT. WAREHOUSE LOADING BAY - CONTINUOUS

[[1:20]] [[P: open choice 7 — shielded wall. Draft uses option A.]] A few steps along the wall, a deep arched loading bay cuts into the warehouse. Inside the arch, the white does not reach: the back wall of the bay is dim, bare, lime-washed.

Ida sets the lantern down on the cobbles a few steps from that back wall, just inside the arch. She and Cas step past it: Ida stops halfway, near the flame; Cas goes on almost to the wall.

On the back wall, thrown by the little lantern, two shadows rise side by side, soft: hers, tall — she is close to the flame, so it climbs the wall — hat brim and split coat; his, small, standing right beside him, bobble cap, hands half-raised. Outside the arch, the whole street is white and has no shadows at all.

[[1:27]] Ida looks at the two shadows for a long moment.

[[1:29]] Then she steps out of the arch. At the end of the street the eleventh gas lamp still burns in its dark corner — the only amber left in the city, long shadows all around it. She climbs the ladder to it. Cas follows and holds the ladder with both hands. She pushes her hat brim back. Her hand rests on the valve.

[[1:35]]

IDA
(softly, voice catching)
That's the last one, then. Goodnight, old street. You'll be brighter now. Just... keep a little dark for the ones who need it.

[[1:41]] As she says "brighter", the corner's own electric post — the end of the last cable segment — clicks, flickers twice, and stands white. White floods the corner. The dark and the long shadows are gone; the little flame beside her face goes pale.

[[1:49]] Close: her hand turns the valve. The flame shrinks, turns blue, and goes out. The empty glass. A beat of stillness.

[[1:52]] Wide: nothing else changes. The white does not even flicker.

[[1:54]] At the foot of the ladder she holds the lantern out to Cas. He takes it with both hands.

[[1:55]] He looks around: white everywhere, no corner of shade left on the street. Then he looks at the arch.

[[1:57]] He carries the lantern out of the white and into the dark loading bay, the amber going with him.

[[1:59]] In the dark, he sets it down and crouches. Without being told, he holds his palms to the glass, exactly as she does. One. Two. Three.

In the mouth of the arch, against the white, Ida sees it. She says nothing.

= Scene 6 — The Window. The street is white; one window is not. Ida takes the new time. (2:02.5–2:22.5, 20 s)

EXT. THE CITY - HIGH ANGLE - NIGHT

[[2:02]] The same high view as the opening. The whole city an even, sleepless white. In the middle of it, one small window glows amber.

EXT. ALLEY BELOW CAS'S WINDOW - NIGHT

[[2:06]] A narrow alley between two houses, where the electric light from the street does not reach. Above, Cas's window glows amber.

Ida stands in the alley under it. In the window's faint spill, a short, pale shadow of her lies on the cobbles again, leaning away from the wall.

[[2:09]] She takes out her pocket watch: 9:53. Through the mouth of the alley, above the rooftops, the white clock of the square reads ten o'clock. She looks from one to the other, turns the crown, and moves the minute hand forward — seven minutes — to ten. She closes the watch without tapping it and puts it away. [[P: open choice 9 — staging of the watch reset. Draft uses option A.]]

[[2:15]] She shoulders the ladder and walks out of the alley. At its mouth, her faint shadow thins and is gone in the white.

INT. CAS'S ROOM - NIGHT

[[2:17]] The lantern on the sill. Cas stands between it and the far wall, his back to us, hands raised. On the plaster, a large, soft bird opens its wings and flies across the wall.

[[2:22]]

> FADE OUT.

> END
